 "text": "If you have to decide to do it, one day you'll decide not to. Why do we stop doing things we know are good for us, reason 15? Say you win that argument 9 days out of 10, sounds great, but after a week the odds you haven't skipped yet are about half, after a month 4%. So stop deciding every day, decide once in one sentence. After something I already do I do the step. After lunch I walk 15 minutes. After I brush my teeth I read 10 pages, after coffee 10 minutes of guitar. The habit you already have becomes the reminder so there's nothing left to argue about, send this to the friend who's always deciding to start. That's what demure is for, archive your dream today and break it into small steps you can actually do. Follow for one reason a day, next why you forget your own goal by week 3. What habit could you hang yours on, the link's in the bio.",
